One Important Improvement ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Our current implementation has one subtle issue.

Suppose

number = 10

Even Thread

prints

10

Now

number = 11

calls

notifyAll();

Odd Thread wakes.

Good.

But suppose Odd Thread was already waiting in

while(number % 2 == 0){
    wait();
}

When it wakes, it should first check:

Has work already finished?

A more robust implementation is:

while (number % 2 == 0 && number <= limit) {
    wait();
}

and after waking, if number > limit, exit immediately.

This avoids any possibility of waiting forever at the end of execution.

Interview Questions ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Q1. Why only one OddEvenPrinter object?

Because both threads must synchronize on the same monitor and share the same number.

Q2. Why use start() instead of run()?

start() creates a new thread of execution. Calling run() directly is just a normal method call on the current thread.

Q3. Does notifyAll() release the monitor?

❌ No.

It only wakes waiting threads.

The monitor is released only when the synchronized block/method exits or the thread calls wait().

Q4. Why doesn't wait() cause a deadlock?

Because wait() releases the monitor before suspending the thread.

Q5. Why do both methods have to be synchronized?

Because both access the same shared mutable state (number). Using the same monitor ensures only one thread manipulates it at a time.


